A||||5||DecadenceR||Alternator... should i replace?||||1||
Z||000000||DecadenceR||06-05-2001||02:41 PM||||I just want to know how i can tell I'm looking for a new alternator... before it actually cuts out. Is there any way?<P>I'm a firm believer in "preventive maintenance"...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||Voytek||06-05-2001||02:46 PM||||The only things you can really check are the carbon brushes inside (2 of them touching the commutator).  If they're very short, replace them.  The other thing is listen to the way it spins, maybe even take off the belt and see if there is any play (bearings).<BR>Actually, you can also check the output withe a volt meter.  I don't remember what it's supposed to be but if it's low, that's a good indication that a change is coming.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||06-05-2001||03:12 PM||||brushes on an altinator?! are you sure about that? on a generator yes - I thought an altinator has the coils on the outside and the part that spins... I forget now<P>  anyway, before you start your car up the battery is around 12.3 to 12.8 volts. After you start it up it should be at 13.8 to 14.8 or so, and then as the charge from the starting current is restored to the battery, it will drop back down to 13 volts or so.<P>  But they can fail in lots of ways. Bearings self destruct - sometimes with a godaweful squealing racket- regulation fails and boils all the water out of your battery  (usually your headlights seems really bright when this happens and you smell rotten egg hydrogen gas odors) diode fail and the battery voltage gets lower and lower till the ECM or ignition shuts down. <P>  if the fan belt starts slipping and squealing when its tight, thats a bad sign too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Voytek||06-06-2001||12:35 PM||||Yup, Ken.  I've rebuilt a couple of Fiero alternators.  There are brushes inside.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||EdsB52||06-06-2001||12:49 PM||||Definately brushes. An alternator performs the same general function as a generator. They are both a starter in reverse. Most devices that transform electrical energy into mechanical motion, or the reverse need brushes, I beleive to complete the ground circuit. Anyway, you can replace the diodes, voltage regulator, bearings, and brushes in GM alternators without any special tools. Now Ford, which such supreme to all, are pressed together and I don't knwo if the internals are available. Just remember, when you go back together with it, pin the brushes back into the retracted position before joining the halves, then pull the pins. It's a piece of cake.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DRH||06-06-2001||01:46 PM||||Most, if not all, car alternators have brushes. They are used to get a current to the windings on the rotor. Varying this current controls the strength of the magnetic field generated by the rotor. That in turn determines how much current is generated in the stator.<P>A lot of motorcycle alternators have a fixed magnet for a rotor and have no brushes. They regulate voltage by sending the excess to ground thru a large resistor with a heat sink.<P>If it's the original and you are getting into the 100,000 mile range, chances are it will go soon. It could act up first with noise and/or falling voltage at idle or the battery light could just come on one day with no warning. If you're worried about getting stranded or having to work on it at an inconvienent time and have the money/time now go ahead and replace it or rebuild it yourself.<P>||||reg||1
